
A master fortran routine that drives the interface (or dirtectly callable
from python is required). This is the program that generate the .pyf 
file (if one desire to have multiple FORTRAN entry points, then all of 
them need a .pyf file and the correspinding FORTRAN routine). The 
.pyf can be created directly or by 

f2py -h <filename.pyf> -m <filename> <options> <fortran files>  (-m flag
specify the module name).

For advanced options https://numpy.org/doc/stable/f2py/usage.html

It is prefarable that Fortran files are in F90 or above. Since the intent,
type specification of arguments must be specified. Examples of a .pyf
file 

!    -*- f90 -*-
! Note: the context of this file is case sensitive.

python module add ! in
    interface  ! in :add
        subroutine add(a,b,n) ! in :add:add.f
            double precision dimension(n), intent(inout) :: a
            double precision dimension(n),depend(n), intent(in):: b
            integer*8 optional,check(len(a)>=n),depend(a), intent(n):: n=len(a)
        end subroutine add
    end interface
end python module add

! This file was auto-generated with f2py (version:2).
! See http://cens.ioc.ee/projects/f2py2e/

Note the integer*8. All our codes are compiled with -i8 (64bit integer) flags.
Unless we specificaly decalare integers are 64  bits all the dependent routines
fails. It seems the default numpy integers are 64 bits since I did not need to
create integers with 64 bit flags (numpy.int8). 

All the dependent routines (that are not directly called by python but 
accessed through the master FORTRAN files must be compiled with standard
way with new flags -shared -fPIC. For example routine in ACES II core 
libraries (acescore,acesutil,tools, lb(for blas and lapack)) compiled as


ifort -c -Wall -traceback -shared -fPIC -O2 -Zp8 -zero -i8 *.f 

(all the facilitites of standard FORTRN can be used, including include
files, directives etc). This step is simply handle by the makefiles 
(cmake).

Then we need to build a shared archive *.so (instead static .a archive)
I used the folliwing command to built the .so archive but I am sure 
there are better ways (similar to ar command for *.a archives)

ifort  -shared -fPIC -o libacescore.so *.o

These steps need to happen within the makefile framework.
We can inspect the archive by
nm -D  libacescore.so 
The results appeare like 

0000000000003710 T aces_auxcache_flush_
0000000000003800 T aces_auxcache_ldblk_
0000000000003e00 T aces_auxcache_reset_
0000000000007090 T aces_bd_aces_reflag_
listing for all the routines in acescore 

Now the building the extension module:

f2py -c --fcompiler=intel -m add add.pyf add.f subtract.so

or more when you have multiple *.so archives 

f2py -c --fcompiler=intel -m aces2py aces2.pyf aces2.F aces2_main.so  aces_libs.so libacescore.s
o libacesutil.so libtools.so liblb.so

(--fcompiler tells it to use the Intel compiler since the default is gfortran)
When this is done we ends up with files

pyscf_2aces2.cpython-36m-x86_64-linux-gnu.so
add.cpython-36m-x86_64-linux-gnu.so 

The name of the extension module is add or pyscf_2aces2. The rest tell us 
about the system and apprently convention from python congress. 

These modules can be imported in python (like we import numpy)

Important to set LD_LIBRARY_PATH as 
export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/home/perera/Develop/ACESII/acespy/libso:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH
 (for shared objects)

export PYTHONPATH=/home/perera/Develop/ACESII/acespy/aces2:$PYTHONPATH
(for pyf generated shared objects). These seems to act like .py files but
unreadble. 




